    "result_text": "Done. All changes are complete and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel`:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` schema (string enum `GOLD`/`SILVER`/`BRONZE`) with `membershipLevel`, a read-only `int32` (min 1, max 4) documenting the algorithm: start at 1, +1 for an email, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3, with level 4 reserved for tenure. This drives the generated `OwnerDto`, so `membershipTier` (and its `MembershipTierEnum`) is gone from the response and `membershipLevel` takes its place.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Replaced the tier expression with the level computation:\n   `Math.min(3, 1 + (email present ? 1 : 0) + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))`.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreNumericLevel`) creates a uniquely-named owner (namesakeCount 0) with an email and expects `membershipLevel == 3` (1 + email + namesake0, capped at 3) \u2014 which now passes.\n\n**On the audit line:** the task noted \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" I checked \u2014 the create audit line here logs `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`, and never recorded the tier; a full grep confirmed the only place referencing the tier in the whole codebase was the mapper. So there was nothing recording the tier to convert, and I left the audit line unchanged.",
